X2Go on macOS — black window interiors and rendering artifacts
===============================================================

Symptom
-------

On macOS (X2Go Client + XQuartz), a connected session shows:

  * the desktop wallpaper and window *decorations* (title bars, borders) render
    correctly, but
  * window *interiors* are solid black (terminals, file managers, app content),
    and
  * moving or raising a window leaves cascading black "trails"/ghost rectangles
    that are never repainted.

This is a long-standing, well-known issue when displaying X2Go/NX sessions
through XQuartz. It is NOT a bug in the session applications and it is NOT the
"could not connect / no window" problem.


Root cause
----------

Two independent factors combine:

1. Remote window-manager compositing (the main cause).
   Modern desktop window managers (XFCE's xfwm4, KDE's KWin, MATE's Marco with
   compositing, GNOME/mutter, Compiz, ...) run a built-in compositor that
   redirects every window to an off-screen pixmap and composites them on screen
   using the X Composite + RENDER extensions.

   That compositing path does not survive the round trip

       desktop apps  ->  x2goagent (nxagent)  ->  nxproxy  ->  XQuartz

   so the composited window contents arrive as black, and damage/expose
   tracking for moved windows is lost (hence the trails).

2. XQuartz 2.8.5 expose regression (secondary).
   XQuartz 2.8.5 (beta3 and later) regressed expose-event handling on resize:
   newly exposed areas are not redrawn. XQuartz 2.8.4 was the last good
   release. This adds resize/move artifacts on top of factor 1, independently
   of X2Go.
   See: https://github.com/XQuartz/XQuartz/issues/309


The fix
-------

Disable the *remote* desktop's compositor (server side, inside the session).
This is the actual fix and is confirmed to make window interiors and window
moves render correctly.

XFCE (xfwm4):

    xfconf-query -c xfwm4 -p /general/use_compositing -s false

  or, in the GUI:
    Settings -> Window Manager Tweaks -> Compositor ->
    uncheck "Enable display compositing"

  or persistently (no running session required), in
  ~/.config/xfce4/xfconf/xfce-perchannel-xml/xfwm4.xml on the X2Go server:

    <property name="use_compositing" type="bool" value="false"/>

Other desktops:
  * MATE:   gsettings set org.mate.Marco.general compositing-manager false
  * KDE:    System Settings -> Display -> Compositor -> disable, or run the
            session with compositing off.
  * GNOME:  use a non-compositing / "classic" or fallback session where
            available.

To apply this for every user of an X2Go server, set the equivalent default
system-wide (e.g. an xfwm4 default in /etc/xdg/xfce4/...).

If resize artifacts persist after disabling remote compositing, you are hitting
factor 2 — downgrade XQuartz from 2.8.5 to 2.8.4.


What NOT to do
--------------

Do not disable the RENDER extension on XQuartz:

    defaults write org.xquartz.X11 enable_render_extension 0   # DO NOT (for X2Go)

This is a popular fix for *direct* X11/Java apps on Apple Silicon
(see https://www.technomancer.com/archives/663), but it breaks X2Go: nxproxy
*replays* the session's RENDER operations onto the local XQuartz server, so
removing RENDER there tears down the proxy with:

    Error: Failure reading from the peer proxy.
    Loop: PANIC! No shutdown of proxy link performed by remote proxy.
    Error: Connection with remote peer broken.


How to verify rendering (screenshots look black even when it works)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

macOS `screencapture` cannot grab XQuartz windows without Screen-Recording
permission, so a working session can still *look* black in a screenshot. Read
the real pixels straight from the X server instead:

    DISPLAY=:0 xwd -name "X2GO-<session-name>" -out /tmp/win.xwd
    xwdtopnm /tmp/win.xwd | pnmtopng > /tmp/win.png     # netpbm
    open /tmp/win.png

(`xlsclients` on :0 shows the running session's window name.)


